WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.901
Metadata filters, and the order of operations.

2
00:00:03.181 --> 00:00:14.232
Real queries carry constraints that no embedding can express: only documents from this product line, only policies effective this year, only content this user is allowed to see.

3
00:00:14.512 --> 00:00:22.576
Qdrant’s documentation states the point plainly: filters exist because you can’t put every property of an object into a vector.

4
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:33.971
Vector databases and search engines let you attach structured fields (often called payload or metadata) and filter on them, and they recommend indexing the fields you filter on.

5
00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:35.936
Where the filter runs matters.

6
00:00:36.216 --> 00:00:42.061
Post-filtering retrieves the top k by similarity and then drops the ones that fail the filter.

7
00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:50.661
If the filter is selective, you may be left with two results out of ten, or none, even though plenty of matching documents exist further down.

8
00:00:50.941 --> 00:01:02.760
Filtering inside the search (restricting the candidates as the index is searched) avoids that, but approximate nearest neighbor indexes can lose recall when a filter removes most of the graph.

9
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:19.488
Qdrant’s 2019 engineering article on filterable HNSW describes this failure and its fixes; modern engines handle it in various ways, so read your engine’s docs on filtered search and test recall with your most selective filters.

10
00:01:19.768 --> 00:01:24.227
In hybrid search, apply the same filters to both retrievers before fusion.

11
00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:33.701
If only the vector side is filtered, the BM25 side can bring back documents the filter was meant to exclude, and fusion will happily rank them.

12
00:01:33.981 --> 00:01:39.784
That leads to the most important rule in this section: permissions are filters, enforced in code.

13
00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:45.269
Never retrieve everything and ask the model to ignore documents the user may not see.

14
00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:51.373
The model reads whatever is in its context, and anything in its context can end up in its answer.

15
00:01:51.653 --> 00:02:02.469
OWASP’s Top 10 for LLM applications lists sensitive information disclosure among its main risks, and retrieval without access control is a direct path to it.

16
00:02:02.749 --> 00:02:13.992
Test this explicitly: a user without access runs a query whose best answer is in a restricted document, and the test asserts that document never appears in the retrieved set.
